4. Shim 清单 (shim symbol list)
-------------------------------

Everything the ported kernels used to get from `server.h`, `zmalloc.h`,
`redisassert.h`, `atomicvar.h` and `object.h`. Nothing here changes behaviour:
each entry is either the upstream definition copied verbatim, a documented
stand-in for server-core state the kernels only read, or an abort for a code
path no redislib facade can reach.

`redislib_port.h` -- macros:
    zmalloc / zrealloc / zfree            -> skynet_malloc / skynet_realloc /
                                             skynet_free (`skynet_malloc.h`)
    serverAssert(_e)                      -> assert(_e)
    serverAssertWithInfo(_c,_o,_e)        -> assert(_e)
    serverPanic(...)                      -> redislib_panic(...)  [noreturn]
    serverLog(...)                        -> no-op
    debugAssert / debugServerAssert / debugServerAssertWithInfo -> no-op
                                            (matches a non DEBUG_ASSERTIONS build)
    REDISLIB_NORETURN                     -> __attribute__((noreturn)) /
                                             __declspec(noreturn)
    redisAtomic, atomicIncr, atomicIncrGet, atomicGetIncr, atomicDecr, atomicGet,
    atomicSet, atomicGetWithSync, atomicSetWithSync
                                          -> copied verbatim from the
                                             "atomic-builtin" branch of
                                             src/atomicvar.h
    OBJ_STRING..OBJ_GCRA (9), OBJ_ENCODING_RAW..OBJ_ENCODING_SLICED_ARRAY (14)
                                          -> values copied from server.h /
                                             object.h
    C_OK, C_ERR, min, max, UNUSED         -> server.h
    ZSKIPLIST_MAXLEVEL, ZSKIPLIST_P, ZSKIPLIST_MAX_SEARCH -> server.h:656-658
    random() / srandom()                  -> redislib_random/redislib_srandom,
                                             only under `#if defined(_WIN32)`
                                             (MSVCRT has neither; Redis uses them
                                             only for skiplist levels and dict
                                             sampling, so a xorshift64* with the
                                             same [0, RAND_MAX] contract is used)
    mkdir(p,m) / O_NONBLOCK / fsync(fd)   -> _mkdir(p) / 0 / _commit(fd),
                                             only under `#if defined(_WIN32)`
                                             (the sole call sites are util.c's
                                             dirCreateIfMissing / dirRemove /
                                             redis_fsync users — filesystem
                                             helpers no redislib facade reaches;
                                             mapped so the verbatim kernels
                                             compile on MinGW-w64)
    One further edit inside a kernel file, for LLP64 portability (MinGW build
    error): util.c getRandomBytes() fallback reseed line casts the FILE* `fp`
    `(long)` -> `(uintptr_t)` (util.c:975). `long` is 32-bit on Windows, so the
    original truncates the pointer; the xor-fed seed value is otherwise
    unchanged, and on POSIX `long` == `intptr_t` width so behaviour there is
    bit-identical. No other line in any ported file differs from upstream
    beyond the three mechanical edit types.

`redislib_port.h` -- types:
    struct robj { unsigned type:4; unsigned encoding:4; void *ptr; }
                                          -> the three members the ported kernels
                                             read; refcount/lru/metabits from
                                             object.h:102 are intentionally not
                                             ported because a redislib object is
                                             owned by the facade that created it
    redislibServerConfig  (extern `server`) -> hll_sparse_max_bytes 3000,
                                               zset_max_listpack_entries 128,
                                               zset_max_listpack_value 64,
                                               memory_tracking_enabled 0;
                                               defaults from src/config.c
    redislibSharedStruct  (extern `shared`) -> minstring / maxstring sentinels
                                               created by redislib_init(), the
                                               zslParseLexRangeItem() unbounded
                                               range edges

`redislib_port.c` -- allocator wrappers (src/zmalloc.h API, skynet has no
    calloc/usable-size variants):
    zcalloc, ztrycalloc, ztrymalloc, ztryrealloc, zmalloc_usable, zcalloc_usable,
    ztrymalloc_usable, ztrycalloc_usable, zrealloc_usable, ztryrealloc_usable,
    zfree_usable, zfree_with_size, zmalloc_usable_size
        (usable size is reported as the requested size: SDS then simply assumes no
         slack and reallocs a little earlier -- no behaviour change)

`redislib_port.c` -- object factories (copied verbatim from upstream, which is
